/*! \page page_outputs Code outputs

    \tableofcontents
    \latexonly
        \textit{\scriptsize Footnotes are situated at the end of the chapter}
    \endlatexonly

    This page describes the various outputs that PB3D and POST can produce.

    On the one hand, there are various text output files, which are discussed in section \ref output_files.

    Furthermore, a multitude of plots can be produced. This is the topic of section \ref output_plots.

    \section output_files Output Files

    Some of these files are opened in open_output(), but others are opened afterwards.

    \subsection output_file_log Log file
    Both PB3D and POST produce a log file, in addition to what is produced on the screen.

    These log files are called <tt>PB3D_out.txt</tt> and <tt>POST_out.txt</tt> and they contain the same information as the output on the screen, but without the special formatting provided by the foul library (see \ref installation_introduction).
    When PB3D continues from an earlier run (<tt>rich_restart_lvl</tt> larger than 1), it appends to <tt>PB3D_out.txt</tt> instead of replacing it.

    \subsection output_file_shell Shell commands file
    Some output routines make use of use_execute_command_line() in order to execute shell commands.
    This can be, for example, the creation of a plot by calling GnuPlot, among other things.

    However, this often does not work well by default (see \ref fni30 "30" in \ref page_inputs).
    In PB3D and POST, therefore, by default, these commands are not executed, but written to the output files <tt>PB3D_shell_commands.sh</tt> and <tt>POST_shell_commands.sh</tt>.
    After PB3D or POST has finished, this file can be executed.

    This behavior can be changed using the <tt>--do_execute_command_line</tt> flag (see \ref page_inputs).


    \subsection output_file_HDF5 HDF5 output file
    PB3D writes variables to the HDF5 output file <tt>PB3D_out.h5</tt> during execution, as shown in table \ref output_file_HDF5_tab "1".
    Note that there is a difference between the equilibrium styles because of the way how the equilibrium codes provide output:
        - VMEC (<tt>eq_style = 1</tt>) produces double Fourier series:
            - This can be evaluated at arbitrary angular points.
            - In PB3D, the equilibrium and perturbation variables are therefore already calculated on the field-aligned grid.
            - Parallel integration can then be done directly.
        - HELENA (<tt>eq_style = 2</tt>) produces 2-D tables for the poloidal cross-section.
            - This needs to be interpolated along the magnetic field lines before parallel integration.
            - The equilibrium and perturbation variables are calculated first in the HELENA grid.

    A group name that ends in <tt>_R_[lvl]</tt> is written for every Richardson level <tt>[lvl]</tt>.
    For the grids, a Richardson level above 1 only holds the parallel points that are new at that level, and POST combines the levels (see reconstruct_pb3d_grid()).
    The level \c rich_restart_lvl is 1 unless PB3D continues from an earlier run.

    <table>
    <caption id="output_file_HDF5_tab">Table 1. HDF5 output file</caption>
    <tr>
        <th width="24%"> group </th><th width="38%"> VMEC (<tt>eq_style = 1</tt>) </th><th width="38%"> HELENA (<tt>eq_style = 2</tt>) </th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> <tt>in</tt>: input variables and equilibrium code output (see print_output_in()) </td><td colspan="2"> written once, in the input driver, if <tt>rich_restart_lvl = 1</tt>; for VMEC it includes the Fourier coefficients on both the main and the Nyquist mode lists (<tt>mn_V</tt> and <tt>mn_V_nyq</tt>) </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> <tt>grid_eq</tt>: equilibrium grid of grid_vars.grid_type </td><td> <tt>grid_eq_R_[lvl]</tt>, field-aligned, at every Richardson level </td><td> <tt>grid_eq</tt>, the HELENA output grid, written once at the level \c rich_restart_lvl </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> <tt>grid_eq_B</tt>: field-aligned equilibrium grid </td><td> not written: the equilibrium grid is field-aligned </td><td> <tt>grid_eq_B_R_[lvl]</tt>, at every Richardson level </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> <tt>grid_X</tt>: perturbation grid </td><td> <tt>grid_X_R_[lvl]</tt>, field-aligned, at every Richardson level </td><td> <tt>grid_X</tt>, in the HELENA output grid, written once at the level \c rich_restart_lvl </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> <tt>grid_X_B</tt>: field-aligned perturbation grid </td><td> not written: the perturbation grid is field-aligned </td><td> <tt>grid_X_B_R_[lvl]</tt>, at every Richardson level </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> <tt>grid_sol</tt>: solution grid </td><td colspan="2"> written once, at Richardson level 1 </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> <tt>eq_1</tt>: flux equilibrium quantities of eq_vars.eq_1_type </td><td colspan="2"> written once, at Richardson level 1 </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> <tt>eq_2</tt>: metric equilibrium quantities of eq_vars.eq_2_type </td><td> not written: POST calculates them again from the Fourier series </td><td> written once, at Richardson level 1, in the HELENA output grid; used later for interpolation to the field-aligned grid </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> <tt>X_1</tt>: vectorial perturbation quantities of x_vars.x_1_type </td><td> not written: POST calculates them again </td><td> written once, at the level \c rich_restart_lvl, in the HELENA output grid; used later for interpolation to the field-aligned grid </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> <tt>X_2_int</tt>: magnetic integrals of the tensorial perturbation quantities of x_vars.x_2_type </td><td colspan="2"> <tt>X_2_int_R_[lvl]</tt>, at every Richardson level, after the last equilibrium job </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> <tt>vac</tt>: vacuum quantities of vac_vars.vac_type, among which the vacuum response <tt>RE_res</tt>, <tt>IM_res</tt> (only if <tt>BC_style(2)</tt> is not 1) </td><td> <tt>vac_R_[lvl]</tt>, at every Richardson level </td><td> <tt>vac</tt>, written once at the level \c rich_restart_lvl </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td> <tt>sol</tt>: solution of sol_vars.sol_type </td><td colspan="2"> <tt>sol_R_[lvl]</tt>, at every Richardson level </td>
    </tr>
    </table>

    The matrices \f$\overline{\text{G}}\f$ and \f$\overline{\text{H}}\f$ of the vacuum boundary-element method are not written; PB3D calculates them again when it continues from an earlier run (see print_output_vac()).

    This data is used for Richardson restart (see variable \c rich_restart_lvl in \ref page_inputs) and for <tt>--jump_to_sol</tt>.

    In the post-processing phase, POST reads this data as well.

    POST, and PB3D when it continues from an earlier run, check the version of PB3D that wrote the file.
    If that version is older than the minimum version set by \c min_PB3D_version in num_vars, they stop with the message <tt>The output is from PB3D version [version]: need at least version [min_PB3D_version]</tt> (see reconstruct_pb3d_in()).


    \subsection output_file_EV Eigenvalue summary file
    In each Richardson extrapolation level, after the solution of the eigenvalue system of equations, PB3D writes the eigenvalues to the text file <tt>PB3D_out_EV_R_[lvl].txt</tt>, where <tt>[lvl]</tt> is the Richardson level.
    This is done in the procedure store_results():
        - The header says whether the eigenvalues are normalized. For VMEC it gives the normalization frequency in Hz, for HELENA it refers to the MISHKA normalization.
        - Every line holds the index of the eigenvalue, its real part, its imaginary part and the relative error that SLEPc gives for it.
        - An eigenvalue is marked by a <tt>#</tt> at the start of its line, followed by a warning line, in three cases.
        The first case is an unphysical complex eigenvalue: the absolute value of the ratio of its imaginary part to its real part is larger than 10 times the SLEPc tolerance \c tol_SLEPC of that Richardson level.
        The second case is an eigenvalue that lies within a relative distance of \f$10^{-3}\f$ from the artificial eigenvalue \c EV_BC that the boundary conditions introduce.
        The third case is an eigenvalue larger than \f$10^{40}\f$ in absolute value; all the following eigenvalues are then discarded as well.
        - The marked eigenvalues are removed from the solution, unless the option \c retain_all_sol is set (see \ref inputs_PB3D_file).


    \subsection output_file_E_rec Energy reconstruction file
    If energy reconstruction is performed by POST (see \c plot_E_rec in \ref page_inputs), the individual terms that make up the perturbed potential energy as well as the kinetic energy of the perturbation are calculated and plotted. (\ref fno3 "3")
    Furthermore, the integrals of these quantities over the whole volume are written to the text file <tt>POST_out_EN_R[lvl].txt</tt>, where <tt>[lvl]</tt> is the Richardson level of the PB3D output that POST uses.
    For every eigenvalue, the file holds a line with the index of the eigenvalue, followed by three pairs of lines; the first line of a pair holds the real parts and the second line the imaginary parts of:
        - the eigenvalue, the ratio \f$E_\text{pot}/E_\text{kin}\f$, \f$E_\text{pot}\f$ and \f$E_\text{kin}\f$,
        - the normal and geodesic kinetic terms,
        - the normal and geodesic line bending terms, the normal and geodesic ballooning terms, the normal and geodesic kink terms, and the vacuum term.

    In table \ref e_rec_tab "2", an overview is given of the different terms.
    Here, \f$\rho\f$ is the density, \f$\sigma = \frac{\vec{B}\cdot\vec{J}}{B^2}\f$ is the parallel current and \f$\kappa = \frac{\vec{B}}{B} \cdot \nabla \frac{\vec{B}}{B}\f$ is the magnetic curvature. See calc_e().

    <table>
        <caption id="e_rec_tab">Table 2. energy reconstruction</caption>
        <tr> <th>potential energy \f$E_\text{pot}\f$ </th><th> kinetic energy \f$E_\text{kin}\f$ </th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td> <ul>
                <li>normal line bending term \f$\frac{1}{\left|\nabla\psi\right|^2 }\left(\frac{\nabla \psi}{\left|\nabla \psi\right|^2}\cdot \vec{Q}\right)^2\f$ </li>
                <li>geodesic line bending term \f$\mathcal{J}^2\frac{\left|\nabla \psi\right|^2}{B^2} \left( \frac{\nabla \psi \times \vec{B}}{B^2}\cdot \vec{Q} \right)^2\f$ </li>
                <li>normal ballooning term \f$-2 p' \left(\nabla \psi \cdot \vec{\xi}\right)^2 \left(\nabla \psi \cdot \vec{\kappa}\right)\f$ </li>
                <li>geodesic ballooning term \f$-2 p' \left( \nabla \psi \cdot \vec{\xi} \right) \left( \frac{\nabla \psi \times \vec{B}}{\left|\nabla \psi \right|^2} \cdot \vec{\xi}\right)^* \left(\frac{\nabla \psi \times \vec{B}}{\left|\nabla \psi \right|^2} \cdot \vec{\kappa}\right)\f$ </li>
                <li>normal kink term \f$-2 \sigma \left(\nabla \psi \cdot \vec{\xi}\right)^* \left(\frac{\nabla \psi \times \vec{B}}{B^2}\cdot \vec{Q} \right)\f$ </li>
                <li>geodesic kink term \f$ 2 \sigma \left(\frac{\nabla \psi \times \vec{B}}{B^2} \cdot \vec{\xi}\right)^* \left(\nabla \psi\cdot \vec{Q} \right)\f$ </li>
                <li>vacuum term \f$\vec{X}_\text{e}^\dagger \overline{\text{R}} \vec{X}_\text{e}\f$, where \f$\vec{X}_\text{e}\f$ holds the Fourier coefficients of the eigenvector at the last point of the solution grid and \f$\overline{\text{R}}\f$ is the vacuum response matrix in the <tt>vac</tt> group of the HDF5 output; this term is zero for a fixed boundary (<tt>BC_style(2) = 1</tt>) </li>
            </ul> </td>
            <td> <ul>
                <li>normal kinetic term \f$\frac{1}{\left|\nabla\psi\right|^2 }\left(\frac{\nabla \psi}{\left|\nabla \psi\right|^2}\cdot \vec{\xi}\right)^2\f$ </li>
                <li>geodesic kinetic term \f$\mathcal{J}^2\frac{\left|\nabla \psi\right|^2}{B^2} \left( \frac{\nabla \psi \times \vec{B}}{B^2}\cdot \vec{\xi} \right)^2\f$; this term is zero for <tt>K_style = 2</tt> </li>
            </ul> </td>
        </tr>
    </table>


    \subsection output_file_mem Memory usage file
    The memory usage of PB3D and POST can be monitored using the optional input argument <tt>--mem_info</tt> (see \ref inputs_PB3D_cmd).
    The memory usage is then written to the files <tt>PB3D_mem_usage.dat</tt> and <tt>POST_mem_usage.dat</tt>, with for every entry the MPI rank, a counter, the time, the memory usage and the maximum total and perturbation memory.

    This is a rather crude implementation and more advanced profiling methods should be used, such as <a href="http://www.scalasca.org/">Scalasca</a> on top of <a href="http://www.vi-hps.org/projects/score-p/">Score-P</a>.

    \ldebug


    \subsection output_file_check_vac Vacuum check output
    If the PB3D input \c check_vac is set for a free boundary, calc_vac_res() tests the assembled vacuum operators with potentials whose boundary values are known exactly, before it solves for the vacuum response (see check_vac_exact()).
    The results are written to the log file, on lines that start with <tt>CHECKVAC</tt>.
    The values per boundary point are written to the files <tt>check_vac_*.dat</tt> in the run directory, with the index of the point, its index along the field line, the index of the field line, its position and the value.
    The values of the exterior potential away from the boundary are written to <tt>check_vac_exterior.dat</tt>.

    \ldebug


    \section output_plots Output Plots
    A multitude of plots can be produced by PB3D and POST.

    POST can produce all the plots that PB3D can, and some more.

    The plots themselves fall in two categories:
        - Plots using an external program.
            - Produced by output_ops.print_ex_2d() or output_ops.print_ex_3d() combined with draw_ex().
            - A list of external programs is given in draw_ex().
            - The data files are written in <tt>Data/</tt>, the scripts in <tt>Scripts/</tt> and the resulting plots in <tt>Plots/</tt>.
            - These plots require a shell command to be run, which is disabled by default and the commands are stored in a \ref output_file_shell.
            - The produced plots can be 2-D or 3-D, and they can be interactive or not, depending on the \c ex_plot_style (see \ref page_inputs).
        - Data files that can be used to produce plots with visualization software:
            - The software used here was <a href="https://visit.llnl.gov/">VisIt</a>, based on personal preference, but <a href="https://www.paraview.org/">ParaView</a> or other VTK-based solutions should work equally well.
            - Every plot creates two output files in <tt>Data/</tt>: one heavy-data HDF5 file and one <a href="https://www.xdmf.org/">XDMF</a> file that contains specifics about the HDF5 file.
            - This is necessary, as HDF5 itself is too general a format for general visualization tools to interpret easily.
            - The XDMF file that accompanies each HDF5 file basically contains information about the saved variables, such as whether they are vectors or scalars, and on which grid type they are defined.
            - This is the file that should be opened by the visualization software.
            - The dimensions of these plots can be 2-D or 3-D, depending on \c eq_style. See \ref fni19 "19" and \ref fni20 "20" in \ref page_inputs.

    An overview of possible plots is now given in table \ref output_plots_tab "3".
    The name of the respective input parameter is provided as well. See \ref page_inputs.

    <table>
